.TH CSTRING_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstring_destroy \- destroy a string and set its pointer to NULL
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstring_destroy(s);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro destroys the cstring. It frees the internal buffer and the
container itself and then sets the pointer variable s to NULL, which
makes a second call safe.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s   cstr lvalue to destroy, such as a variable or an element of an
    array; the macro evaluates it exactly once and sets it to NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A call with a NULL pointer is safe and does nothing.
The internal buffer and the container are freed with the allocator that
created the string, which is the custom ccol_memmgmt_procs_t given to
cstring_create_full(), or free() when none was given.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr s = cstring_create("hello", NULL);
cstring_destroy(s);   /* s is now NULL */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstr_destroy (3)
